Agent Action ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Summary: Add explicit Tomcat dependencies to dependencyManagement in root 
pom.xml

Reasoning:
Right, so I've been looking at the `osv-scan-results.json` file, and it's clear 
that the Tomcat vulnerabilities are still hanging around. My initial thought was
that setting `tomcat.version` in the `pom.xml`'s properties section would sort 
this out, but that's not quite how it works when you're dealing with a Spring 
Boot parent POM or a Bill of Materials (BOM) that manages dependencies.

What's actually happening is that the `tomcat-embed-core` dependency, which is 
the key component for Tomcat within Spring Boot, is still being pulled in at 
version `11.0.21`. This is why those pesky vulnerabilities, specifically 
`CVE-2026-65905` and `CVE-2026-41293`, are showing up.

To get around this, I realize I need to be more explicit. When using a Spring 
Boot parent, simply setting the `tomcat.version` property isn't enough to 
override the version managed by the parent. I need to go into the 
`dependencyManagement` section of my *root* `pom.xml` and add explicit 
dependency declarations for the Tomcat components. This way, I'm forcing all 
modules that inherit from this root POM to use the version I specify.

I've identified that the critical vulnerabilities are:

*   **`CVE-2026-65905` (CRITICAL):** This is an Authentication Bypass in the 
DIGEST authenticator. The affected versions range from `11.0.0-M1` up to 
`11.0.24`, `10.1.0-M1` up to `10.1.57`, and `9.0.0.M1` up to `9.0.120`. The fix 
versions are `11.0.25`, `10.1.58`, and `9.0.121`.
*   **`CVE-2026-41293` (CRITICAL):** This relates to HTTP/2 request headers not 
being validated. The affected versions are `11.0.0-M1` to `11.0.21`, `10.1.0-M1`
to `10.1.54`, and `9.0.0.M1` to `9.0.117`. The fix versions are `11.0.22`, 
`10.1.55`, and `9.0.118`.

My goal is to address both of these. I've already set `tomcat.version` to 
`11.0.25`, which is the current stable version that resolves both these 
vulnerabilities.

Now, the crucial step is to go back into the `dependencyManagement` section of 
the root `pom.xml` and explicitly declare the Tomcat dependencies. I'll be 
targeting `tomcat-embed-core`, `tomcat-embed-websocket`, and `tomcat-embed-el`, 
and setting their versions to `11.0.25`. This is the most robust way to ensure 
all our sub-modules are correctly using the patched Tomcat version. So, yes, 
it's time for another pass at that root `pom.xml`.

